  (text: "Outdoor scene parsing models typically achieve high performance when trained on curated, ideal datasets; however, their efficacy often degrades when applied to unconstrained real-world environments. Specifically, the quality of scene parsing\u2014and sky classification in particular\u2014diminishes significantly in images characterized by nighttime illumination, diverse weather conditions, and seasonal variations. To address these challenges, this study utilizes the state-of-the-art RefineNet architecture in conjunction with challenging, non-ideal data sources: the SkyFinder dataset and a subset of the SUN database containing sky regions. We focus specifically on improving binary sky segmentation to distinguish between sky and non-sky pixels more accurately. Our results demonstrate a 10\u201315% improvement in the average Misclassification Rate (MCR) compared to prior methods on the SkyFinder dataset. Furthermore, we achieved a nearly 35% increase in mean Intersection over Union (mIoU) relative to the off-the-shelf baseline. Finally, an analysis of our models regarding time of day and weather conditions reveals that, despite facing the same environmental difficulties as previous methods, our approach yields significantly superior performance.", aiProbability: 0.973421),
  (text: "A significant challenge in machine learning is the discrepancy between training datasets and the real-world data encountered during deployment. In the context of language modeling, user-generated content\u2014such as private messaging\u2014evolves over time and may diverge significantly from the static distributions observed in publicly available corpora. Nevertheless, public data remains essential for establishing foundational linguistic knowledge. This study investigates strategies for the distributed fine-tuning of a general model on private user data, subject to the constraints of preserving performance on the general domain and minimizing communication overhead. We introduce a novel technique that substantially enhances prediction accuracy on user-specific language relative to a general model and surpasses gradient compression methods in communication efficiency. The proposed method is computationally efficient, achieving a nearly 70% reduction in perplexity and an 8.7 percentage point increase in the keystroke saving rate for informal English. Furthermore, we demonstrate that the applicability of our approach extends beyond language modeling tasks. Finally, we present an experimental framework for assessing the differential privacy of distributed training, showing that our method offers robust privacy guarantees.", aiProbability: 0.975052),
